(a) Any person who is charged with an offense against any of the provisions of this Code or any ordinance of the city shall:
(1) Be informed by summons, in writing, served on him of the nature and cause of the accusation;
(2) Have compulsory process for obtaining witnesses in his behalf;
(3) Have a speedy trial;
(4) Be confronted with the witnesses against him; and
(5) Have the privilege of cross examination, as in the superior courts of the state.
(b) The same rules apply as to examination of witnesses and the evidence adduced as obtained in such courts, as far as they are applicable to cases before the municipal court. The person accused shall have the privilege of defending himself, by counsel or by himself, or both, whichever shall seem proper to him. No one shall be condemned, fined, or punished without a chance of being heard in his defense.

542:
(a) It shall be unlawful for any person to:
(1) Intentionally, and without authority, injure or destroy any meter, pipe, conduit, wire, line, post, lamp, or other apparatus belonging to a company, the city, other political subdivision, or governmental entity which is engaged in the manufacturing or sale of electricity, gas, water, telephone, cable, or other public services;
(2) Intentionally, and without authority, prevent a meter from properly registering the quantity of such service supplied;
(3) In any way, interfere with the proper action of such company, municipality, or political subdivision;
(4) Intentionally divert any services of such company, municipality, or political subdivision; or
(5) Otherwise, intentionally and without authority, use or cause to be used, without the consent of the company, municipality, or political subdivision, any service manufactured, sold, or distributed by the company, municipality, or political subdivision.
(b) Where there is no evidence to the contrary, the person performing any of the illegal acts set forth in subsection (a) of this section and the person who, with knowledge of such violation, receives the benefit of such service without proper charge as a result of the improper action shall be presumed to be responsible for the act of tampering or diversion.
(c) Proof that any of the acts specified in subsection (a) of this section were done on premises in possession of the accused or that the accused received the benefit of any such act shall be prima facie evidence that the accused committed such act, or aided or abetted in the commission thereof.
(d) Each day in which these acts occur or in which some person receives the benefit of any such acts shall be cumulatively and considered separate violations of this section.
(Ord. No. 500, 3-20-1989)

